      "Language": "pol",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The authors present a diagnostically difficult case of a three year old girl with abdominal pain. The girl with abdominal pains, nausea, upper airways infarction and some urinary system symptoms was admitted to Children's Surgical Clinic for observation. She was given antibiotic therapy and i.v. infusions. WBC was 29.6 tys./ul and CRP 2.7 mg/dl. No other abnormalities were detected in biochemical or sonographic investigation. The girl was submitted to laparotomy because of unclear abdominal signs suggesting acute appendicitis. Phlegmonous appendicitis and twisted/rotated left ovary with multiple adhesions were found. Histopathological investigation showed teratoma of the left ovary. Postoperative course went without complication."},
